  {
    "episode_id": 49786,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49786.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Exactly. Which tells me they're not interested in the truth, right? I also would send it to me. But even beyond that, this story goes back to January the 5th. which was just another day in Congress. I mean, I had no idea what was gonna transpire on the six as far as riots or threats to the Capitol. I know that some did, as we're finding out now, through intelligence reports, there were some people that did know that there were threats to the Capitol. I had no idea. My colleagues have told us we had no idea. So it was just another day. We had some constituents from the 11th district that were in Washington, D.C. to attend this rally at the White House for then outgoing President Trump. Some people we have met at a church sometime earlier, they had actually helped campaign for us in my reelection, the last reelection. And so they had told us, look, we're coming to Washington, D.C. If it's at all possible, I'd love to see your office. And so as we have done hundreds of times in the past when constituents come to Washington, they sure, you know, we'll, we'll get you into the office and, you know, we'll visit for a little while. And the only thing that's different was that there were COVID restrictions. So. There wasn't just open public access to the house office buildings. There was limited access. Basically meaning you could still have guests, but somebody from our office actually had to go up front and meet them. They go through the metal detectors and all that, and then you just escort them back to the office. It was COVID restrictions. But there was one specific restriction. No one was allowed into the actual capital building except for members of Congress. their spouse and certain staff, most of my staff could not even get into the capital. It was closed off due to the COVID restrictions. So the family, as they called us after it was a bus, a bus folks from our district traveled to DC for the rally. They called us as they were heading over to meet us and said we had some other people that came up from the district who never met you. Is it okay if they come along as well? I'm like, sure, some friends and family members of theirs. They come to the office. We stay in the office for a period of time just talking and we had originally planned on going to lunch with them. Just pick a restaurant close by and we would go to lunch. However, from the time that we discuss them coming and visiting us. until the actual day, the mayor of DC had closed all the restaurants because of COVID. You weren't able to do dining in. So that kind of thwarted our plans of taking them nearby somewhere to go to lunch. But there is a cafeteria in the basement of the house office buildings. So we could just go down to the cafeteria here. And so that's what we did. We went to the cafeteria afterwards. Um, we took them to the house gift shop, which is right next to the cafeteria in a house office building. And after, um, uh, after they bought some gifts, we ended up, you know, going back to the office. Uh, they left everything's, they're fine. Then of course, all the stuff happens on January six. Well, a few days after January six, um, I received a copy of a complaint, a letter that was actually being sent. by 34 Democrats, they were sending a letter to the Department of Justice, asking the Department of Justice to investigate and open a criminal investigation against Republicans who were conducting reconnaissance tours on January 5th of the Capitol. While I'm on the committee, House Administration is the committee, I'm on the committee that oversees security capital. So this raises a red flag, first of all, There were no tours allowed in the Capitol. Second of all, here is one member making very serious accusations against under other members of Congress, which if unfounded is a violation of House rules. So the committee staff starts investigating it and literally views thousands of hours of video from the security cameras throughout the Capitol. And there were no tours. as she described, no tours much less reconnaissance tours. And I don't know how you tell the difference between a reconnaissance tour and a regular tour, but there were no tours of the capital period.",
